      "text": "First, we find the endpoints of segment $s_1$ using the given midpoint formula. Since the midpoint of a segment is the average of the x-coordinates and y-coordinates of its endpoints, we can set up the following system of equations:\n\\[\n\\frac{(4,1) + (-8,5)}{2} = \\left(\\frac{4+(-8)}{2}, \\frac{1+5}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{-4}{2}, \\frac{6}{2}\\right) = (-2, 3)\n\\]\nTherefore, the endpoints of $s_1$ are $(-4, 2)$ and $(-8,6)$.\nNext, we find the endpoints of $s_2$ by translating $s_1$ by $2$ units to the right and $3$ units up. Using the concept of translation, we can easily find the endpoints of $s_2$:\n\\[\n(-4, 2) + 2 = (-2, 5)\n\\]\n\\[\n(-8, 6) + 2 = (-6, 9)\n\\]\nFinally, we find the midpoint of $s_2$ using the midpoint formula again. By the midpoint formula, the midpoint of a segment with endpoints $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2}\\right)\n\\]\nIn this case, the midpoint of $s_2$ is:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{-2 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{5 + 9}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{-8}{2}, \\frac{14}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-4, 7)}\n\\]\nThe answer is: -4",
